  "worked_solution": "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\theta \\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\theta \u00f7\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta ) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }\u00f7\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\theta })}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{-1} \\\\  & ={1}^{-1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
